# **The movie that found a bug**, and the third of the five spawn entries to get
# an input.
#
# BLHR is level 45 as it starts. The target is the type-$36 object at (866,994)
# -- collision id $2E ($80:CA30), whose jump-table entry is `$80:FA4A`: kind 1,
# and a counter at $7E:1D4C that stops at five.
#
# `$80:FA4A` ends `LDX $0E : LDA $1D4C,X : CMP #$0005 : BCS : INC A : STA`, and
# **the `LDX` is an output as well as an index.** The port had been leaving X as
# whatever `thread_spawn` returned, because the whole tail was transcribed from
# the listing with no input able to run it. First call: `X: ROM $0000, port
# $0022`. Every byte of WRAM matched; only X did not, which is the same shape as
# `thread_spawn`'s own first run. `$80:FA26` had the identical error and no input
# reaches it even now -- it was fixed by reading, not by diffing.
#
# `spawn_count_capped` is still zero: one object is one increment, and the
# ceiling wants five.
#
# **Nothing contests this one, and that took two goes.** A chained route that
# collected the (540,1100) object first arrived here with the player's own
# collision id reading $38 rather than $05 -- level 45's spiders do something to
# him on the way -- and $38 is not one of the three ids `$80:CAEE` lets take an
# object, so he walked over it and nothing happened. The direct route gets here
# in twelve legs with the id still $05.
#
# `zamn_cosim verify -f 3400` checks 54,342 calls with no divergence.
#
# Route: east along the bottom of the map, then north up the x=862 lane.

